### Description

`HOST_TAG_NAME` returns `'#host'` instead of the tag name when the host element isn't declared in a template. That's the case for the root component, components rendered by `<router-outlet>`, components created with `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` or `createComponent()`, directives on those hosts (`hostDirectives` or the `directives` option), and hosts created by `TestBed.createComponent` and `TestBed.createDirective`:

```ts
@Directive({selector: 'a[uiButton], button[uiButton]'})
class UiButton {
  isAnchor = inject(HOST_TAG_NAME) === 'a';
}

const fixture = TestBed.createDirective(UiButton, {tagName: 'a'});
fixture.directiveInstance.isAnchor; // false, HOST_TAG_NAME is '#host'
```

The token returns `tNode.value`. For an element in a template, that's its tag name. But the host TNode that `ComponentFactory` creates for these components always holds the `'#host'` placeholder (`TNodeName.DynamicHost`), since the element is only known at runtime. Host binding sanitization already handles that placeholder by reading the tag from the native element (`resolveElement` in `sanitization.ts`). `HOST_TAG_NAME` doesn't, and has returned the placeholder since it was added in #54751.

Code that switches on the tag name, like the example in the "DI in action" guide, silently falls into its default branch for these components. For example, a directive that emulates `disabled` on `<a>` hosts stops blocking clicks when the link is a component opened in a dialog.

`DebugElement.name` returns the same placeholder for these hosts, so `fixture.debugElement.query((de) => de.name === 'app-page')` doesn't find a routed component, while `By.css('app-page')` does.

#71460 reads the tag name from the native element in both cases.

